  "moduleSlug": "data-management",
  "courseSlug": "microservices-architecture",
  "title": "Database per Service Pattern",
  "description": "Implement the database per service pattern for data isolation.",
  "order": 1,
  "difficulty": "advanced",
  "estimatedMinutes": 19,
  "tags": ["database-per-service", "data-ownership"],
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "The database per service pattern gives each microservice its own database. This ensures data ownership and loose coupling between services."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Key Concepts"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "key-terms",
      "data": {
        "terms": [
          {
            "term": "Database per Service",
            "definition": "Each service owns its own database exclusively."
          },
          {
            "term": "Data Ownership",
            "definition": "The principle that only the owning service accesses its data."
          },
          {
            "term": "Schema Isolation",
            "definition": "Each service has its own schema or database."
          },
          {
            "term": "Polyglot Persistence",
            "definition": "Using different databases for different services."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Pattern Overview"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "Each service has its own database. Other services cannot access the database directly. They must use the service API to access data."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Benefits"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "bullet-list",
      "data": {
        "items": [
          "Loose coupling through data isolation",
          "Each service can choose its own database",
          "Independent scaling of databases",
          "Clear data ownership"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Challenges"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "bullet-list",
      "data": {
        "items": [
          "Cross-service queries are hard",
          "Data duplication may be needed",
          "Distributed transactions are complex",
          "Reporting across services is harder"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Implementation"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "bullet-list",
      "data": {
        "items": [
          "Separate database per service",
          "Separate schema per service (lighter option)",
          "Separate database server per service (strongest isolation)"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Polyglot Persistence"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "data": {
        "text": "Database per service enables polyglot persistence: using the right database for each service. For example, PostgreSQL for transactions, Redis for caching, MongoDB for documents."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "data": {
        "level": 2,
        "text": "Architecture"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "mermaid",
      "data": {
        "id": "msa-db-per-service",
